## 2. Transactions and the UTXO Model

Bitcoin does not maintain account balances in the same manner as a traditional bank ledger.

Instead, Bitcoin uses Unspent Transaction Outputs (UTXOs).

A transaction consumes one or more previously created outputs and creates one or more new outputs. An output remains unspent until it is referenced by a later transaction input.

A simplified transaction flow is:

Previous Transaction
        |
        v
   UTXO Output
        |
        v
   New Transaction
      /       \\
     /         \\
New UTXO     New UTXO

A wallet balance therefore represents the aggregate value of UTXOs controlled by the wallet rather than a single account balance.

### Why the UTXO model matters for analysis

The UTXO model provides analysts with a structured record of:

- Transaction inputs
- Transaction outputs
- Transaction identifiers
- Output indexes
- Amounts
- Transaction fees
- Spending relationships
- Block inclusion
- Transaction timing

These relationships form the foundation for many forms of Bitcoin blockchain analysis.

---

## 3. Transaction Structure

A Bitcoin transaction contains inputs and outputs.

An input references a previous transaction output using:

- Transaction ID (txid)
- Output index (vout)

The transaction also contains authorization data used to demonstrate control over the referenced output.

Outputs specify:

- Amount of bitcoin
- Spending conditions/script

The Bitcoin developer documentation describes each input as spending satoshis previously assigned to an output, while outputs remain UTXOs until subsequently spent.

Source:
https://developer.bitcoin.org/devguide/transactions.html

---

## 4. Cryptographic Authorization

Bitcoin transactions use cryptographic signatures to demonstrate control over funds.

The Bitcoin developer documentation describes the use of the secp256k1 elliptic curve and digital signatures in transaction authorization.

At a conceptual level:

Private Key
    ↓
Digital Signature
    ↓
Transaction Authorization
    ↓
Network Validation

The private key is therefore a critical security boundary.

Loss or compromise of private-key material can result in permanent loss of control over associated assets.

This creates a strong relationship between blockchain technology and traditional cybersecurity principles such as:

- Access control
- Credential protection
- Key management
- Authentication
- Operational security
- Incident response

---

## 5. Blocks and the Blockchain

Bitcoin transactions are organized into blocks.

Each block contains a set of transactions and references the preceding block through information contained in the block header.

This creates a chronological chain of blocks.

Simplified:

Block N-2
   ↓
Block N-1
   ↓
Block N
   ↓
Block N+1

Each new block therefore builds on the history established by previous blocks.

The structure makes historical modification increasingly difficult because changing a previous block would require reconstructing subsequent proof-of-work and competing with the continuing network.

Source:
https://developer.bitcoin.org/devguide/block_chain.html

